This is typically the default and most common configuration\n- The primary CCS handles the management/scheduling duties\n\n**CCS Mode 2:**\n- Uses CCS1 (a different compute control station)\n- An alternative configuration for which CCS handles the control responsibilities\n\n**CCS Mode 4:**\n- This is where it gets interesting \u2014 mode 4 typically involves **using multiple CCS units** or a different distribution of control responsibilities across the compute control stations.\n\n## When to Use Each\n\nThe practical reality is that **these modes are mostly relevant for:**\n\n1. **Debugging and troubleshooting** \u2014 If you're experiencing issues with one CCS over another, or trying to isolate hardware problems\n2. **Performance tuning** \u2014 Different workloads may benefit from different CCS configurations, though this is niche\n3. **Driver/development work** \u2014 Understanding which CCS is active can help diagnose scheduling issues\n\n## Practical Advice\n\nFor **most users, you should leave this on the default setting (mode 1)**. The differences are subtle and:\n\n- Require specific workloads to show any benefit\n- Are primarily useful for developers and advanced debugging\n- Won't meaningfully affect gaming or general compute performance\n\n---\n\n**A note of honesty:** CCS mode specifics can vary between driver versions and exact GPU architectures, and I want to be careful not to give you overly precise details that might be slightly off. If you're working with a specific use case (like a particular compute workload or debugging scenario), let"}